/** 
\mainpage 
Arctic Engine
=============

Getting started from scratch is always the biggest challenge and use of time.
Arctic Engine API is so easy to use it makes it fun to learn, just like it ought to be.

Look how easy it is to use:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "engine/easy.h"
    using namespace arctic;
    
    void EasyMain() {
      while (!IsKeyDownward(kKeyEscape)) {
        Clear();
        DrawCircle(Vec2Si32(100, 100), 50, Rgba(255, 255, 255));
        ShowFrame();
      }
    }
~~~~~

The entry point is `void EasyMain()`, the engine links exactly this signature, so
it can't return an exit code. An application that has one to report (a command
line tool built on the engine, for example) calls
`ExitProgram(exit_code)`, see @ref ExitProgram. Returning from `EasyMain` is the
same as `ExitProgram(0)`.

Features
--------

- Returns the power to the C++ programmer
- Makes game development fun again
- Easy to use
- Quick code-build-run cycle

Installation
------------

### Windows

Install Visual Studio Community 2019. Make sure to select the 'Desktop development with C++' workload. Run Visual Studio and click the 'Clone or check out code' button. Enter 'https://gitlab.com/seaice/arctic.git' as the Repository location. In the solution explorer open the 'wizard' folder and doubleclick the 'wizard.sln'. Click the green triangle 'Local Windows Debugger'.

A 3-minute videoguide:\n
\htmlonly
<iframe width="560" height="315" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/sGG-fGHGwQs" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe>
\endhtmlonly

### Linux

Install some dependencies, clone the source code, make and run the wizard:
~~~~~{.bash}
    cd ~ && sudo apt-get install git cmake clang libasound2-dev libglu1-mesa-dev freeglut3-dev libgles2-mesa-dev && git clone https://gitlab.com/seaice/arctic.git && cd ~/arctic/wizard && cmake . && make -j 4 && ./wizard
~~~~~

### MacOS

Install XCode. Run XCode and select 'Souce Control -> Clone' menu option. Enter 'https://gitlab.com/seaice/arctic.git' in the 'Enter repository URL' field and click 'Clone' buttons. Select 'File -> Open' menu option, open the 'arctic/wizard' directory (by default located in your Documents directory). Click the 'Product -> Run' menu option.

### Building for the web browser

Instructions for Linux and MacOS users on how to run an Arctic Engine game in a web browser:

1. Install Emscripten following the instructions at https://emscripten.org/docs/getting_started/downloads.html
    Don't forget to set the environment variables:
    ~~~~~{.bash}
    source ./emsdk_env.sh
    ~~~~~
2. Navigate to your game directory.
3. Remove the CMake cache:
    ~~~~~{.bash}
    rm -f ./CMakeCache.txt
    ~~~~~
4. Build the project:
    ~~~~~{.bash}
    emcmake cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release . && make -j 8
    ~~~~~
5. Start the web server (the `webserver.py` script is included in projects created with the wizard):
    ~~~~~{.bash}
    python3 ./webserver.py
    ~~~~~
6. Open the address http://127.0.0.1:8000/index.html in your browser.

Creating and updating a project
-------------------------------

Every project is made by the wizard, and there is no other supported way. A copy
of `template_project_name` is not a project: the name of the target, the name of
the bundle, the Xcode and Visual Studio projects, the relative path to the engine
and the list of engine files inside them all have to agree with each other and
with the directory name, and the wizard is what makes them agree. Copying the
template by hand gives a directory that builds under a wrong name, or does not
build at all.

Build the wizard once (see Installation above) and run it with no arguments for
the window, or with a subcommand for a terminal:

~~~~~{.bash}
    wizard create mygame                     # the default template
    wizard create mygame --template hello    # a chosen one
    wizard update ../mygame                  # refresh an existing project
    wizard --help                            # the list of templates
~~~~~

A name is a directory name and a build target name at once, so it takes lowercase
latin letters, digits and underscores, and it starts with a letter. The templates
are `tetramino`, `hello`, `snake`, `kids`, `conquest`, `des` and `cube`; the
`--help` text lists what each one shows. The new project appears next to the
engine directory: `wizard create mygame` from the engine at `~/arctic` gives
`~/mygame`, which is what the relative include path `../arctic` in the generated
project expects.

`update` is for a project made earlier with an older engine. It rewrites the
generated parts, the engine file lists in the Xcode and Visual Studio projects
above all, and leaves your own sources alone. It looks for the engine next to
itself first, so it works from any directory and out of any build, and it
recognises a project by the `<name>.xcodeproj` and `<name>.vcxproj` in it.

A subcommand never opens a window: the wizard tells the engine to start headless
as soon as it sees a command word, so `create` and `update` work over ssh, in a
container and on a build bot, printing what they do and ending with 0 on success
or 1 on a mistake in the arguments.

The assets of a project live in its `data` directory, and the program loads them
by relative paths like `data/hero.tga`. That works because the current directory
is arranged for it before `EasyMain` (see "Command Line Arguments and File Paths"
below): a macOS bundle carries the data in `Contents/Resources`, and a build in a
directory of its own gets a copy of `data` next to the binary.

Build the project in the project directory:

~~~~~{.bash}
    cmake . && make -j 8                          # in the source directory 
~~~~~

The engine is built with clang. CMake takes the compiler from
`-DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=...` or from the `CXX` environment variable when either is
set, otherwise it looks for `clang++` in the PATH, and it stops with a message
naming what to install when there is none. gcc is not supported.

On Linux the sound library is optional: without the ALSA headers the build says
so, defines `ARCTIC_NO_ALSA`, and the program runs mute, telling the log about it
once and answering `SoundPlayer::IsOk()` with false. The graphics backend is
chosen with `-DARCTIC_GRAPHICS=auto|glx|gles`, and `auto` takes desktop OpenGL
through GLX everywhere except a Raspberry Pi, which keeps GLES through EGL; the
configure step prints which one it took. To run with no window, no GL context and
no sound device at all, set `ARCTIC_HEADLESS=1` and `ARCTIC_DISABLE_HW=1` in the
environment; `ARCTIC_DISABLE_AUDIO=1` alone only keeps the sound device closed.

Sample project types
--------------------

Project examples cover a wide range of functionalities, including basic graphic operations, game development, programming education, and event modeling. These examples serve as an excellent starting point for exploring the project's capabilities and inspire the creation of your own applications.

1. Hello World

  This example demonstrates basic text display on the screen. The program loads a font and displays the message "Hello world!" on the screen in a loop until the Escape key is pressed.
  
  Key Features:
  - Loading and using a font.
  - Main game loop with input handling.
  - Rendering text on the screen.

2. Tetramino game

  This example implements a classic falling blocks game where players control various shaped blocks. The program manages falling pieces, processes user input to control blocks, and checks for line completion.

  Key Features:
  - Managing block positioning and rotation.
  - Handling collisions and filled lines.
  - Rendering the game field and the current block.

3. Snake
  
  This example implements the classic Snake game. The program manages snake movement, food collection, and collision detection.

  Key Features:
  - Snake movement and growth mechanics.
  - Collision detection.
  - Score tracking.

4. Coding for Kids
  
  This example is designed to teach the basics of programming. The program provides functions for graphic display of elements, text input, and user interaction.

  Key Features:
  - Rendering basic graphic primitives (points, lines, circles).
  - Sound output.
  - Handling user input and displaying text.
  - Simple command input structure for interacting with the program.

5. Turn-based strategy

  This example implements a simple strategy game where players control various types of units. The game includes territory capture mechanics, player turns, and win condition checks.

  Key Features:
  - Defining types of units and their properties.
  - Main game loop with turn processing and win condition checks.
  - Rendering game elements on the screen.

6. Discrete-event simulator

  This example demonstrates the basics of discrete event simulation. The program manages time-based events, updates the model, and displays results on the screen.

  Key Features:
  - Managing simulation time and events.
  - GUI for managing simulation parameters.
  - Displaying the current state and progress information.

Quick Tutorials
--------------

### Command Line Arguments and File Paths

The current directory of a running Arctic application is not the directory the
user started it from. On macOS the engine makes `<bundle>/Contents/Resources`
current before `EasyMain` is called, so that an application can load its assets
by relative paths without knowing where the bundle was copied to:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    Sprite hero;
    hero.Load("data/hero.tga");  // relative to the bundle resources
~~~~~

That is what you want for assets, and exactly what you do not want for a path
the user typed. `mygame open level.txt` would look for `level.txt` inside the
bundle rather than in the shell's directory. Pass every path that comes from the
command line through `CanonicalizeArgvPath()`:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "engine/easy.h"
    using namespace arctic;

    void EasyMain() {
      if (GetEngine()->GetArgc() >= 2) {
        // Absolute paths are kept as they are; a relative one is resolved
        // against the directory the program was started from, not against the
        // current one. Works the same way on every platform.
        const std::string path =
            CanonicalizeArgvPath(GetEngine()->GetArgv()[1]);
        std::vector<Ui8> data = ReadFile(path.c_str(), true);
        if (data.empty()) {
          *Log() << "can not read " << path;  // says which path was tried
        }
      }
    }
~~~~~

Key path functions:
- `CanonicalizeArgvPath(path)`: absolute canonical form of a path that came from
  argv, resolved against the startup directory
- `GetStartupDirectory()`: the directory the process was started from, captured
  before anything changed the current one; empty on the web
- `GetEngine()->GetInitialPath()`: where the application itself lives (on macOS
  the directory containing the bundle, elsewhere the startup directory), the
  right base for files that belong to the application
- `CanonicalizePath(path)`: canonical form resolved against the *current*
  directory, which is the resources folder inside a macOS bundle
- `GluePath(dir, name)`, `ParentPath(path)`, `RelativePathFromTo(from, to)`:
  string-level path arithmetic that touches no filesystem state
- `DescribeFilePath(path)`: one line naming both spellings of a path and the
  likely reason it could not be opened, for error messages and logs
- `DoesFileExist(path)`, `DoesDirectoryExist(path)`: what is at the path, if
  anything; a directory answers `kTrivalentUnknown` to the file question
- `ChangeCurrentDirectory(path)`, `GetCurrentPath(&dir)`: read and move the
  current directory, remembering that relative asset paths follow it

Two more consequences of the same rule are worth remembering. A relative path
opened for writing lands inside the bundle on macOS, where a rebuild replaces
it, so anything the application saves for the user should be written under a
path of its own choosing rather than a bare relative name. And a custom `main`
built with `ARCTIC_NO_MAIN` never runs the engine's startup code, so it should
call `SetStartupDirectory()` itself if it wants argv paths to resolve;
otherwise `CanonicalizeArgvPath()` falls back to the current directory.

### Console Subcommands and Exit Codes

A subcommand like `mygame convert a.png b.tga` has nothing to draw, and a window
that opens and closes right away is worse than no window at all, on a machine
with a display or without one. The engine asks before it creates the window, the
GL context and the sound device, and the answer comes from a function of yours
registered before `main` runs:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "engine/easy.h"
    using namespace arctic;

    namespace {

    bool WantsHeadlessRun() {
      Engine *engine = GetEngine();  // the command line is already here
      return engine->GetArgc() >= 2 &&
             std::string(engine->GetArgv()[1]) == "convert";
    }

    }  // namespace

    ARCTIC_HEADLESS_DECIDER(WantsHeadlessRun)

    void EasyMain() {
      if (GetEngine()->IsHeadless()) {
        ExitProgram(Convert(CanonicalizeArgvPath(GetEngine()->GetArgv()[2])));
      }
      ResizeScreen(1280, 720);
      ...
    }
~~~~~

Everything else about such a run is the same as usual: the same current
directory, the same log, the same argv rules, and the backbuffer is a piece of
memory nothing presents. Setting both `ARCTIC_HEADLESS` and `ARCTIC_DISABLE_HW`
in the environment asks for it from outside, without a decider.

`ExitProgram(code)` is how a subcommand reports its verdict: the code reaches the
shell, the log is flushed and the sound device is released on the way out. Never
`std::exit` or `std::_Exit` instead, and remember that `EasyMain` is `void` and
can not return a value; returning from it means `ExitProgram(0)`.

### Drawing Text

Text rendering in Arctic Engine is handled by the `Font` class. Here's a simple example of how to load a font and draw text:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "engine/easy.h"
    using namespace arctic;
    
    void EasyMain() {
      // Create and load a font
      Font gameFont;
      gameFont.Load("data/arctic_one_bmf.fnt");
      
      while (!IsKeyDownward(kKeyEscape)) {
        Clear();
        
        // Draw text with different alignments and colors
        // White text at the top-left
        gameFont.Draw("Hello Arctic Engine!", 20, ScreenSize().y - 20, 
                     kTextOriginTop, kTextAlignmentLeft, 
                     kDrawBlendingModeColorize, kFilterNearest, 
                     Rgba(255, 255, 255));
        
        // Yellow text centered on screen
        gameFont.Draw("Centered Text", ScreenSize().x / 2, ScreenSize().y / 2, 
                     kTextOriginCenter, kTextAlignmentCenter, 
                     kDrawBlendingModeColorize, kFilterNearest, 
                     Rgba(255, 255, 0));
        
        ShowFrame();
      }
    }
~~~~~

Key points for text rendering:
- Use `kDrawBlendingModeColorize` for best results with colored text
- Text origin options: `kTextOriginTop`, `kTextOriginBottom`, `kTextOriginCenter`, `kTextOriginFirstBase`
- Text alignment options: `kTextAlignmentLeft`, `kTextAlignmentCenter`, `kTextAlignmentRight`
- `BaseToTop()`, `BaseToBottom()` and `LineHeight()` are the vertical metrics for
  code that places lines itself instead of drawing a multiline string

Text over a picture is unreadable without an outline, and an outline does not
have to cost several Draw calls. Ask for it when loading the font and it is baked
into the glyphs once:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    Font hudFont;
    // two pixel black outline around white glyphs
    hudFont.LoadTtf("data/fonts/MyFont.ttf", 16.0f, nullptr, 0, 2.0f,
                    Rgba(0, 0, 0, 255));

    hudFont.Draw("Readable over noise", 20, 20, kTextOriginBottom,
                 kTextAlignmentLeft, kDrawBlendingModeColorize, kFilterNearest,
                 Rgba(255, 255, 0));
~~~~~

`kDrawBlendingModeColorize` multiplies every glyph pixel by the color passed to
`Draw`, so the white body of the glyph takes that color while the black outline
stays black, whatever color the text is. An outline of some other color would be
tinted by the text color the same way, so bake the text color into the glyphs and
draw with `kDrawBlendingModeAlphaBlend`, which ignores the `Draw` color, when both
colors have to survive exactly. `AddBorder()` does the same to an already loaded
font, and note that a `Font` is a shared handle: bordering one copy borders every
copy made from the same load.

### Processing Mouse Input

Arctic Engine makes it easy to handle mouse input. Here's how to track mouse position and detect clicks:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "engine/easy.h"
    using namespace arctic;
    
    void EasyMain() {
      Font gameFont;
      gameFont.Load("data/arctic_one_bmf.fnt");
      
      // Create a circle parameters
      Vec2Si32 circleCenter(300, 300);
      Si32 circleRadius = 40;
      bool isCirclePressed = false;
      
      while (!IsKeyDownward(kKeyEscape)) {
        Clear();
        
        // Get current mouse position
        Vec2Si32 mousePos = MousePos();
        
        // Check if mouse is over the circle using distance calculation
        Vec2F diff = Vec2F(mousePos - circleCenter);
        bool isMouseOverCircle = Length(diff) <= circleRadius;
        
        // Check for mouse click on circle
        if (isMouseOverCircle && IsKeyDownward(kKeyMouseLeft)) {
            isCirclePressed = true;
        }
        
        // Draw circle (green if pressed, blue if mouse over, gray otherwise)
        Rgba circleColor = isCirclePressed ?
                          (isMouseOverCircle ? Rgba(0, 255, 128) : Rgba(128, 255, 128)) : 
                          (isMouseOverCircle ? Rgba(0, 0, 255) : Rgba(128, 128, 128));
        
        DrawCircle(circleCenter, circleRadius, circleColor);
        
        // Draw text
        gameFont.Draw("Click Me!", circleCenter.x, circleCenter.y,
                     kTextOriginCenter, kTextAlignmentCenter, 
                     kDrawBlendingModeColorize, kFilterNearest, 
                     Rgba(255, 255, 255));
        
        // Display mouse coordinates
        std::string mouseText = "Mouse: (" + std::to_string(mousePos.x) + 
                               ", " + std::to_string(mousePos.y) + ")";
        gameFont.Draw(mouseText.c_str(), 20, 20, kTextOriginBottom, 
                     kTextAlignmentLeft, kDrawBlendingModeColorize, 
                     kFilterNearest, Rgba(255, 255, 255));
        ShowFrame();
      }
    }
~~~~~

Key mouse input functions:
- `MousePos()`: Returns the current mouse position as Vec2Si32
- `MouseMove()`: Returns the mouse movement since last frame
- `MouseWheelDelta()`: Returns the mouse wheel movement
- `IsKeyDownward(kKeyMouseLeft)`: Detects left mouse button press
- `IsKeyDown(kKeyMouseLeft)`: Checks if left mouse button is held down
- `IsKeyUpward(kKeyMouseLeft)`: Detects left mouse button release

### Keyboard Input and Keyboard Layouts

A keyboard answers two different questions, and the engine keeps them apart. The
key functions speak of physical keys, named after the letters a US keyboard prints
on them, so `kKeyW` is the key above the middle of the left hand whatever the
current layout makes of it. `TypedText()` gives what the user actually typed, in
UTF-8, through the layout the user chose:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "engine/easy.h"
    using namespace arctic;

    void EasyMain() {
      Font font;
      font.Load("data/arctic_one_bmf.fnt");
      Vec2Si32 hero(100, 100);
      std::string name;

      while (!IsMainWindowCloseRequested() && !IsKeyDownward(kKeyEscape)) {
        // Physical keys: the same corner of the keyboard in every layout, so
        // this keeps working when the user types in Cyrillic or on AZERTY.
        if (IsKeyDown("w")) { hero.y += 4; }
        if (IsKeyDown("s")) { hero.y -= 4; }
        if (IsKeyDown("a")) { hero.x -= 4; }
        if (IsKeyDown("d")) { hero.x += 4; }

        // Typed text: whatever the layout produced this frame, letters of any
        // alphabet among them.
        name += TypedText();

        Clear();
        DrawCircle(hero, 20, Rgba(255, 255, 255));
        font.Draw(name.c_str(), 20, 20, kTextOriginBottom, kTextAlignmentLeft,
                  kDrawBlendingModeColorize, kFilterNearest,
                  Rgba(255, 255, 255));
        ShowFrame();
      }
    }
~~~~~

Which is to say: hotkeys and movement ask about keys, text asks about text. Never
read `InputMessage::Keyboard::key` to build a string and never call
`IsKeyDown('q')` hoping to catch a typed letter, because on a Cyrillic layout the
key stays `kKeyQ` while the letter is a different one entirely. The message queue
carries both: `.keyboard.key` is the physical key, `.keyboard.characters` is the
UTF-8 text of that one message, which is what `TypedText()` collects for the
frame. The string returned by `TypedText()` lives until the next `ShowFrame()`.

Keys that carry no text carry none anywhere. Tab, Enter, Escape, Backspace and
Control with a letter are reported as characters by the systems underneath, "\t"
and "\r" among them, and the engine leaves all of that out: `.keyboard.characters`
is empty for them, `TypedText()` skips them, and an `Editbox` never puts a
tabulation in its text because the user pressed Tab to move to the next field.
Such a key is read as a key, with `IsKeyDownward(kKeyTab)` and its like.

Keys that are modifiers have a generic form as well as a left and a right one, so
`IsKeyDown(kKeyControl)` is true for either Control key, and the same holds for
Shift and Alt.

### Ending the Program

No key ends anything by itself. A program that leaves on Escape does so because it
asked, which is why every example above has `IsKeyDownward(kKeyEscape)` in the
loop condition rather than trusting the engine to notice.

The window is closed by the user, though, with the red button, with Cmd+Q, with
Alt+F4 or from the window manager, and by default the engine ends the process
right there. An application with something to do first registers a handler and
takes the closing over:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "engine/easy.h"
    using namespace arctic;

    namespace {

    // Atomic because the handler runs on another thread on Windows, see below.
    std::atomic<bool> g_is_leaving{false};

    bool OnClose() {
      if (!HasUnsavedWork()) {
        return true;  // nothing to lose, let the engine close
      }
      g_is_leaving.store(true);  // ask the user about it in the frames to come
      return false;              // and nothing is closed for now
    }

    }  // namespace

    void EasyMain() {
      SetMainWindowCloseHandler(OnClose);
      while (!IsMainWindowCloseRequested() || g_is_leaving.load()) {
        ...
        ShowFrame();
      }
      SaveEverything();
    }
~~~~~

`IsMainWindowCloseRequested()` is the polling form of the same news: it goes from
false to true when the user asks and stays true, so a main loop can end on its own
terms. Without a handler the process ends on the request, and a loop that wants to
see the flag has to register one.

The handler is called on the thread that pumps the window messages, once per
request. On macOS and on Linux the messages are pumped from inside `ShowFrame()`,
so that is the thread `EasyMain` runs on, and the handler is as free with the
application data as the frame itself. On Windows the window belongs to the main
thread while `EasyMain` runs on a thread of its own, so the handler runs beside the
frame and shares data with it; keep it to setting a flag, as the example above
does, and do the work in the loop. A program that registers no handler at all and
only reads `IsMainWindowCloseRequested()` never meets the question, because the
flag is atomic.

### Logging

Arctic Engine provides a convenient streaming interface for logging. Here's how to use it:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "engine/easy.h"
    using namespace arctic;
    
    void EasyMain() {
      // Simple message logging
      *Log() << "Game started!";
      
      // Logging variable value
      float score = 42.5f;
      *Log() << "New high score achieved: " << score;
    }
~~~~~

Key logging features:
- Uses convenient streaming syntax similar to std::cout
- Thread-safe non-blocking lightning-fast logging
- Automatically adds newline at the end of each log message

The streaming form needs a complete `std::ostringstream`. Including
`"engine/easy.h"` gives it to you, but `"engine/log.h"` alone does not: that
header names the stream through `<iosfwd>` to stay cheap to include. A file that
includes only the logger has to ask for the stream explicitly:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include <sstream>
    #include "engine/log.h"
~~~~~

Without `<sstream>` the compiler complains about the `<<` operator with a message
like "invalid operands to binary expression ('std::basic_ostringstream<char>'
and 'const char[13]')". Code that would rather keep its includes light can use
the plain overloads instead, they need no streams at all:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    Log("MeshObj_Read can't open ", file_name);
~~~~~

### Random Number Generation

Arctic Engine provides simple functions for generating random numbers. Here's how to use them:

~~~~~{.cpp}
      // Random integers
      Si32 randomInt = Random32(1, 100);  // Random integer between 1 and 100 (inclusive)
      
      // Random floating point numbers
      float randomFloat = RandomF();      // Random float in range [0.0, 1.0)
      float randomValue = RandomF() * 10.0f;  // Random float in range [0.0, 10.0)
      
      // Random signed float in range [-1.0, 1.0)
      float randomSignedFloat = RandomSF();
      
      // Random direction vector
      float angle = RandomF() * 2.0f * 3.14159f;  // Random angle in radians
      Vec2F direction(cos(angle), sin(angle));    // Random direction vector
      
      // Random color
      Rgba randomColor(Random8(), Random8(), Random8());
      
      // Random position on screen
      Vec2Si32 screenSize = ScreenSize();
      Vec2Si32 randomPosition(
          Random32(0, screenSize.x - 1),
          Random32(0, screenSize.y - 1));
~~~~~

Key random number generation features:
- `Random(min, max)`: Returns a random Si64 in range [min, max]
- `Random32(min, max)`: Returns a random Si32 in range [min, max]
- `RandomF()`: Returns a random float in range [0.0, 1.0)
- `RandomSF()`: Returns a random float in range [-1.0, 1.0)
- `RandomD()`: Returns a random double in range [0.0, 1.0)
- `RandomSD()`: Returns a random double in range [-1.0, 1.0)

### Playing Sounds

Arctic Engine makes it easy to load and play sound effects and music. Here's how to use the sound system:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "engine/easy.h"
    using namespace arctic;
    
    void EasyMain() {
      // Load sound effects
      Sound explosionSound;
      explosionSound.Load("data/explosion.wav");
      
      Sound backgroundMusic;
      backgroundMusic.Load("data/music.ogg");
      
      // Play background music
      backgroundMusic.Play(0.5f);  // Play at 50% volume
      
      // Game loop
      while (!IsKeyDownward(kKeyEscape)) {
        Clear();
        
        // Play explosion sound on space key press
        if (IsKeyDownward(kKeySpace)) {
          explosionSound.Play(0.8f);  // Play at 80% volume
        }
        
        // Restart background music if it stopped playing
        if (!backgroundMusic.IsPlaying()) {
          backgroundMusic.Play(0.5f);
        }
        
        ShowFrame();
      }
    }
~~~~~

Key sound features:
- Load WAV and OGG files with `Sound::Load()`
- Play sounds with `Sound::Play()`
- Control volume by passing a parameter to `Play(float volume)`
- Check if a sound is playing with `SoundHandle::IsPlaying()`

### Creating GUI Buttons

Arctic Engine provides a simple yet powerful GUI system for creating interactive interfaces. Here's how to create and use buttons in your game:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "engine/easy.h"
    #include "engine/gui.h"
    using namespace arctic;

    Rgba g_backgroundColor = Rgba(0, 0, 50);

    /// @brief Callback invoked when the button is clicked.
    void OnClick() {
      *Log() << "Button was clicked!";
      g_backgroundColor = Rgba(Random8(), Random8(), Random8());
    }
    
    void EasyMain() {
      ResizeScreen(1280, 720);
      
      // Create GUI factory
      GuiFactory guiFactory;
      
      // Load a theme
      guiFactory.theme_ = std::make_shared<GuiTheme>();
      guiFactory.theme_->Load("data/gui_theme.xml");
      
      // Create a root panel to hold GUI elements
      std::shared_ptr<Panel> gui = std::make_shared<Panel>(-1, Vec2Si32(0, 0), Vec2Si32(0, 0));
      
      // Create a button
      std::shared_ptr<Button> button = guiFactory.MakeButton();
      button->SetPos(Vec2Si32(100, 100));
      button->SetSize(Vec2Si32(200, 50));
      button->SetText("Click Me!");
      button->OnButtonClick = OnClick;

      // Add the button to the root panel
      gui->AddChild(button);
      
      
      // Game loop
      while (!IsKeyDown(kKeyEscape)) {
        Clear(g_backgroundColor);
        
        // Process input for GUI
        bool isInputHandled = false;
        for (Si32 i = 0; i < InputMessageCount(); ++i) {
          isInputHandled = gui->ApplyInput(GetInputMessage(i), nullptr) || isInputHandled;
        }
        
        // Draw GUI
        gui->Draw(Vec2Si32(0, 0));
        
        ShowFrame();
      }
    }
~~~~~

Key GUI features:
- Use `GuiFactory` to create GUI elements like buttons, panels, and text fields
- Customize appearance with themes loaded from XML files and image atlases
- Organize GUI elements in a hierarchy
- Handle user interactions with callbacks
- Process input with `ApplyInput()` method
- Draw GUI elements with `Draw()` method

An application with hotkeys of its own has one more thing to do: the keys must
not fire while the user is typing into a field. Ask the root panel whether the
focus is inside something that reads the keyboard, and follow an edit box through
its callbacks rather than by polling `GetText()`:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    std::shared_ptr<Editbox> rate = guiFactory.MakeEditbox();
    rate->OnTextChange = OnRateTyped;   // typing, deleting, pasting, undo, redo
    rate->OnEditDone = OnRateAccepted;  // Enter, or the focus went elsewhere
    gui->AddChild(rate);

    // ... in the game loop, after ApplyInput:
    if (!gui->IsKeyboardCaptured()) {
      if (IsKeyDownward(kKeySpace)) {
        TogglePause();
      }
    }
~~~~~

`Panel::IsFocused()` answers the same question about one particular panel, and
the queue passed to `ApplyInput` carries `kGuiEditboxTextChange` and
`kGuiEditboxEditDone` for a host that prefers messages to callbacks. The
callbacks run in the middle of input handling, so they must not add or remove
panels; remember what to do and do it after the input loop.

### Localization

Arctic Engine provides an ICU MessageFormat-compatible localization system for multi-language game support:

~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "engine/easy.h"
    #include "engine/localization.h"
    using namespace arctic;
    
    void EasyMain() {
      // Load localization strings from CSV file
      // CSV format: id,en,ru,de (or id,en for single language)
      Localization::Instance().Load("data/strings.csv");
      Localization::Instance().SetLocale("en");
      
      Font font;
      font.Load("data/font.fnt");
      
      while (!IsKeyDownward(kKeyEscape)) {
        Clear();
        
        // Simple string lookup
        font.Draw(Loc("hello").c_str(), 100, 300);
        
        // String with variable substitution
        std::string greeting = Loc("greeting", {{"name", "Player"}});
        font.Draw(greeting.c_str(), 100, 250);
        
        // Plural forms (automatically selects correct form based on locale)
        std::string items = Loc("items", {{"count", 5}});
        font.Draw(items.c_str(), 100, 200);
        
        // Gender selection
        std::string pronoun = Loc("pronoun", {{"gender", "female"}});
        font.Draw(pronoun.c_str(), 100, 150);
        
        ShowFrame();
      }
    }
~~~~~

Example CSV file (strings.csv):
~~~~~
id,en,ru
hello,Hello World,Привет Мир
greeting,Hello {name}!,Привет {name}!
items,"{count, plural, one {# item} other {# items}}","{count, plural, one {# предмет} few {# предмета} many {# предметов} other {# предметов}}"
pronoun,"{gender, select, male {He} female {She} other {They}}","{gender, select, male {Он} female {Она} other {Они}}"
~~~~~

Key localization features:
- Load strings from UTF-8 CSV files with automatic locale detection from column headers
- Simple variable substitution: `{name}` replaced with provided value
- ICU plural forms with locale-specific rules (one/few/many/other)
- Select expressions for gender and other categorical choices
- Ordinal numbers: `{n, selectordinal, one {#st} two {#nd} few {#rd} other {#th}}`
- Nested expressions: plural inside select and vice versa
- Fallback locale support for missing translations
- Merge multiple CSV files for modular localization

